00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:18.630 align:start position:0%
 
change the world  so  it's my turn  um i think there's we've heard a lot of  great talks today but i think no day at  the gsb would be complete without a talk  about entrepreneurship so here i am and  here we all are at stanford in the heart  of silicon valley and if you think and  if you've been thinking that more and  more startups seem to be starting up  every day  you're right  according to kyle schrum who's the head  of the kaufman foundation  we now live in the most entrepreneurial  time in history  just think about that for a second  and i am part of the most  entrepreneurial generation  so  i want to start by sharing a story  another case study from this most  entrepreneurial generation of line  it's the story of 24 year old timmy an  entrepreneur i met this summer when i  was running a program to help him start  his business  now  timmy did everything right he  found a real customer need he devised an  innovative distribution method for his  product and it just went gangbusters he  sold out the very first day he launched  he sold out in two hours and doubled his  money and now he's on the track to  expanding his business and creating jobs  so that's pretty much a textbook case of  the successful young entrepreneur story  right  and it is  but here's the thing it didn't happen  here in palo alto  but here  in lagos nigeria  now lagos is one of the world's fastest  growing mega cities  seventy percent of the population lives  in slums and sixty percent of the  economic activity is in the informal  sector  timmy's full name is timalehan akinpelu  he grew up in a polygamous family where  the three wives of his father and all  their children crowded into one small  room  his product was not some sort of  ecommerce luxury thing but rather hand  sanitizer affordably and attractively  distributed in recycled water bottles  and his customers  were these folks trash pickers  homeless migrants who crowd the  sprawling slums of this mega city  rooting through trash with their bare  hands under the sun every day  so  timmy's probably not the typical  entrepreneur right he's not he's not the  typical person we think of when we think  of the successful young  business person starting some sort of  world-changing venture  but i argue that we should think more  about timmy and invest in folks like him  so  the reason why is that 15 only 15 of my  entrepreneurial generation are in rooms  like this one on campuses like this one  the other  85 percent  um  are in developing countries  they are youth crowded into the slums of  these desperate mega cities  stuck in informal sector work that's  often precarious and exploitative  and for them the only mainstream stories  they hear about youth making it  are just that  fantasies stories like this one slumdog  millionaire this movie about a street  youth who makes it big and wins the  grand prize  in a game show that's kind of like who  wants to be a millionaire millionaire so  you know i'm all for fortune and fate  and game shows but the truth is these  aren't real solutions for the majority  of the 85 percent  and this majority is increasingly poor  disenfranchised and desperate  i think we need to find a better way to  put slum youth on the track to becoming  slum millionaires  and i think the way to do this is by  tapping our entrepreneurial generation  that's what i've been doing for the last  three years in the slums of lagos  working with youth like timmy to start  businesses  and what i want to talk about with you  today is why the 85 percent matters  what how how we go about tapping their  incredible world-changing potential  and what we can all do what help i need  from you to make this happen  so let's start with the why  it's about youth  so it turns out that not only am i part  of the most entrepreneurial generation  but also the most populous and the most  precarious  according to the world bank we're the  largest youth cohort in human history  and we are leading the largest mass  migration in human history from the the  world's rural areas to urban centers  and employers just can't absorb the  flood of desperate hungry restless youth  seeking jobs that they don't find in  these urban centers  as a result  there are now 80 million young people  who are unemployed  another 150 million people on top of  that  are living very desperate marginal  livelihoods in the informal sector for  less than two dollars a day  youth now make up half of the unemployed  people on this planet  and as i already told you 85 percent of  these youth and all the problems that  come with them  are in the developing world the places  that need their talents and energy the  most  so  that's probably the first reason you  should care about the 85 frankly the 85  percent has the potential to be very  scary  first of all it's a it's a social  justice issue it's an economic issue and  it's an international security issue  because disenfranchised  youth living in poverty are prime  recruiting grounds for international  terrorists and local criminal  organizations  they cost their countries and local  economies millions if not billions in  lost potential and productivity  and in the informal sector where they're  trapped they're very vulnerable to  exploitation  the second reason you should care is  that they actually represent a really  big untapped opportunity it turns out if  that if we're able to cut the youth  unemployment in sub-saharan africa by  half  we would actually boost regional gdp by  12 to 19 percent that's huge  moreover folks like ck prahlad here  contend that there's a large untapped  market here at the bottom of the pyramid  a multi a multi-trillion dollar fortune  at the bottom of the pyramid that's up  for grabs for anyone who can see the  poor not as victims or beneficiaries but  as customers and collaborators  the last reason i have to care deeply  about the 85 percent is pretty personal  um as you know i've been working oops  sorry as you know i've been working at  the bottom of the pyramid with  my peers in the 85 percent  and i've got a big group of global kind  of youth volunteers that are focused on  eking out the opportunities that we know  exist in this population  now  we reject labels like lost generation  and ticking time bomb that are often  used to describe this 85 percent  instead we call ourselves generation  enterprise  we think that generation enterprise is  the only term that truly captures the  hope and resilience that these youth  bring in terms of their life experience  and their dreams  when it comes to building a better  future for themselves and their  communities  and i have to say i have learned at  least as much as i've taught my peers in  the 85 percent and i truly believe that  this spirit of friendship and  collaboration is actually a humbler more  inclusive frankly better way to do  economic development work in the 21st  century  also it's very tough not to feel deeply  connected to my peers when they send me  flip cam videos like the one i'm about  to show you  so  where were we so that's now that we've  established why we should care about the  85 percent  let's talk about generation enterprise  and what we're doing about it  so we've developed a model that focuses  on  uh tapping this potential in the 85 a  model that gets youth off the streets  and running businesses  this is how it works so it all starts  with empathy we partner with local  non-profits that refer youth to us and  we listen to their stories  we're screening them to be candidates in  our tightly knitted small cohorts of  generation enterprise fellows  now what we look for two things the  first is actions they've taken to  improve their lives this could be  something like escaping prostitution or  it could be as simple as seeking  vocational training  we also look for actions they've taken  to care for others so they often  manifest themselves in either family  situations or being the leaders of a  local gang  next what we do is we uh we focus on  these stories that allow them to tap  their deep personal experiences to truly  uncover customer needs in the  communities they hope to serve with  business solutions so the story i want  to share is one of bosch who's right  here so he came to us from freedom  foundation which was an ngo that  operates  safe houses for ex-gang members ex-drug  addicts and excess workers  and  he came to us at a point when he was  actually living in this one room shack  made of scraps of metal and wood and he  was working these odd jobs in local  churches to support his grandmother and  his three sisters because his parents  were gone  so  his dream was to build a real house for  his grandma and send his sisters to  school  and he was a perfect gen fellow because  folks like him folks like bash are  really eager to prove themselves and  work really hard and they also tap their  deep personal experiences to uncover  these community needs  uh bosch ended up you know anyone you  talk to here the design school at  stanford we'll talk about how deep  empathy  is at the heart  of design thinking  and bash was able to use these design  thinking tools to design his own  business which captures uh he is a  photography business that captures  family moments and community events  the second thing we do is take small  bets so after training our generation  enterprise fellows we then make small  bets on them and their ideas we give  them a mere ten dollars which they can  use to just test the most basic version  of their business ideas they pool their  funds and go through a series of  business experiments that help them  understand whether they're tapping a  true consumer need in a way that's  valuable enough for people to pay for  and the reason why that works is it's  about small bets and small bets are the  best way to fail fast get smarter for  cheap right and you know whether you're  talking about starbucks retail format or  google's pagerank algorithm  big breakthroughs come out of small bets  and anyone here in silicon valley will  tell you  that  you know  business plans don't survive their first  contact with reality what you need to do  is get out of the building and test it  and so that's what two of our business  two of our generation enterprise fellows  did this summer uh judith and olumide  ended up trying to tackle the problem of  nutrition in slums  so it turns out that whether you're  living in a gay gay lagos or the bronx  in new york city the poor you are the  poor you eat  and there's actually research that shows  that if you give a poor person a dollar  for food they're going to spend it on  sugar because something sugary because  almost biochemically their bodies crave  the comfort so judith and olumide had a  big problem cut out for them how do you  make healthy food attractive and  affordable to the poor in slums and so  they went through a series of different  experiments until they hit the jackpot  by learning that they could serve  individual packaged  individual serving sized fruit salads  refreshingly chilled and delivered to  you while you work  and the reason why they knew they'd hit  the jackpot and that it was time to make  a bigger bet was that they started  selling 30 packs a day  the last thing we do finally is launch  the full business so at this point we've  finished the test phase and we're  basically we've basically figured out  what most venture capitalists wish they  could do which is put their potential  investees under a microscope for four  months every day work alongside them and  understand how they're serving their  customers how they're managing their  money how they're refining their  businesses  so at this point we've already already  shaken out the best ideas and the most  viable  and hardworking youth and we then put in  a larger investment of up to a thousand  dollars so i know that's large is all  relative but here it's really large it's  a thousand dollars to more fully build  out the business and successful fellows  then reach out to us and are able to  channel their profits back to generation  enterprise as well as create at least  five other jobs for at-risk homeless and  unemployed youth in their communities  so what we're essentially doing here  is we're taking traditional venture  capital  into the slums  turning it on its head and giving it a  bit more heart  because traditional venture capital  basically involves looking for but  ultimately waiting for the best  entrepreneurs to match with the best  ideas and come to you so you can decide  whether to invest or not  what we do is we start with the right  youth the right entrepreneurs we  co-create the right businesses with them  and then we go big and we go into  business together and for us this is the  most sustainable solution to solving  this problem of youth unemployment  so now we come to the end what can you  do to help  now  the answer this is simple i want you to  join me in playing a game of who wants  to back a slum millionaire  so there's three steps to this it's very  simple to play the first one is share  what you know  now uh  we've already run three pilots in lagos  we've worked with 45 youth and launched  14  low-tech consumer brands in the slums  now what we're looking to do is scale we  know we've got something that works here  but there are a lot of questions scaling  is really tough i mean how do you mass  produce startups  how do you find the right business  models  you know how do you who do you work with  who can we partner with  these are all questions i need your help  answering and so i ask you today to  actually email me at  claragenerationenterprise.org  think about who we should talk to who in  your contacts what in your experience  can help us get to scale  the second is to make small bets again  it didn't take a million dollars or a  game show to take timmy or judith or  lumiere out of poverty and give them  choices and dignity  so i'm asking you for 10 bucks  this is all it takes to actually test in  business to make sure it's viable  and then if it is then we'll make we'll  figure out how to make the bigger  investment  the last thing to do is if you're really  brave launch an incubator generation  enterprise has been invited to 15  different countries from india to  colombia to cameroon we can't be in all  those places at once but we want to  reach these youth so if you're willing  join us as a team member come on as a  board member or launch your own  incubator and share what you're learning  when it comes to making slum  millionaires  so i want to finish by just going back  to that slum millionaire in the making  timmy remember him he's the guy from  timmy soap  and his business is growing rapidly he  could expand to cover all the different  zones in the city of lagos and all the  thousands of trash pickers who could use  his services he could become the bath  and body works of nigeria  no matter what happens though  he is making money that will keep him  off the streets allow him to start a  family hire others and frankly become a  respected and contributing member of his  community  and really that's where we want to get  to i think that's the goal  because even if timmy falls short of  becoming a slum millionaire  at the end of the day timmy his family  his employees and all of us  who care about this entrepreneurial  generation  we'll have one  thank you  you

